module

public import Mathlib.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.ChangeOfRings
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Determinant
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Dual.Lemmas
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Block
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.AdjoinRoot
public import StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Dualizing.SelfDualAlgebra

/-!
# A monogenic algebra is its own `R`-linear dual

Let `R` be a commutative ring and let `B` be an `R`-algebra carrying a power basis
`1, y, …, y^{d-1}`.  Then `B` is self-dual over `R`: the `B`-module `Hom_R(B, R)` is free of
rank one, generated by the functional `δ_{d-1}` reading off the coefficient of `y^{d-1}`.

The mechanism is that `δ_{d-1}` makes multiplication into a perfect pairing: the matrix of
`x ↦ δ_{d-1}(x · -)` from the power basis to the *reversed* dual basis is upper triangular with
`1` on the diagonal, because `δ_{d-1}(y^{i+j})` is `1` when `i + j = d - 1` and vanishes when
`i + j < d - 1`.  Its determinant is therefore a unit and the pairing is nondegenerate.

The case of interest is `B = R[x]/(f)` for a monic `f`, which is `AdjoinRoot f`; there `d` is the
degree of `f` and the power basis is `Mathlib`'s `Polynomial.Monic.powerBasis'`.

## Main results

* `Dualizing.powerBasis_bijective_mul_topCoord` — for a power basis of positive dimension, the
  pairing `(x, z) ↦ δ_{d-1}(x * z)` is perfect, i.e. `x ↦ δ_{d-1}(x · -)` is a bijection
  `B → Hom_R(B, R)`.
* `Dualizing.powerBasis_exists_bijective_mul_compr₂` — the same with the functional existentially
  quantified, so that the degenerate case `d = 0` (where `B` is trivial) is covered too.
* `Dualizing.powerBasisSelfDualIso` — **`B ≅ Hom_R(B, R)` as `B`-modules** for any `B` with a
  power basis, stated with `Hom_R(B, R)` in the form in which derived coextension of scalars
  meets it, `ModuleCat.coextendScalars (algebraMap R B)` applied to `R`.
* `Dualizing.adjoinRootSelfDualIso` — **`R[x]/(f) ≅ Hom_R(R[x]/(f), R)` as `R[x]/(f)`-modules for
  a monic `f`**.  This is the generator `δ_{d-1}` of the proof of [Stacks, Tag 0C0G]; see the
  implementation notes.

## Implementation notes

`AdjoinRoot f` *is* `R[X] ⧸ Ideal.span {f}`, so `Dualizing.adjoinRootSelfDualIso` is literally the
self-duality of `R[x]/(f)`.

The `R`-linear dual appears as
`(ModuleCat.coextendScalars (algebraMap R B)).obj (ModuleCat.of R R)` rather than as
`Module.Dual R B`, because `Mathlib` puts the `B`-module structure of `Hom_R(B, R)` — the one
coming from the `B`-action on the *source* — only on the former. That is also the shape in which
the statement is consumed: `ModuleCat.coextendScalars` is the underlying functor of the derived
coextension of scalars `RHom_R(B, -)` of [Stacks, Tag 0A70].

This forces `R` and `B` into a single universe: the carrier of
`(ModuleCat.coextendScalars (algebraMap R B)).obj (ModuleCat.of R R)` lives in `max u_R u_B`,
which must be `u_B` for the two sides of the isomorphism to be comparable.

Why this statement and not the trace form: over a general commutative ring the trace pairing of a
monogenic algebra is *degenerate* — already for `R = 𝔽_p` and `f = x^p` — so `Algebra.traceForm`
cannot produce the generator, and the top-coefficient functional is used instead.  Nothing here
needs `R` Noetherian, `B` separable, or `f` squarefree.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0C0G](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0C0G)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0BZE](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BZE)
-/

@[expose] public section

open CategoryTheory Module

universe u

namespace Dualizing

variable {R B : Type u} [CommRing R] [CommRing B] [Algebra R B]

/-- **The top-coefficient pairing of a power basis is perfect.**  Let `b` be a power basis of
`B` over `R` of dimension `d > 0` and let `δ_{d-1} : B →ₗ[R] R` be the `b`-coordinate of index
`d - 1`.  Then `x ↦ δ_{d-1}(x · -)` is a bijection from `B` onto `Hom_R(B, R)`.

The matrix of this map from `b` to the dual basis of `b` reversed is upper triangular with ones
on the diagonal: its `(i, j)` entry is `δ_{d-1}(y^{j + (d - 1 - i)})`, which is `1` exactly when
`j = i` and is `0` when `j < i`, since then the exponent is below `d - 1` but still `< d`. -/
theorem powerBasis_bijective_mul_topCoord (b : PowerBasis R B) (hd : 0 < b.dim) :
    Function.Bijective ((LinearMap.mul R B).compr₂
      (b.basis.coord ⟨b.dim - 1, Nat.sub_lt hd (by omega)⟩)) := by
  classical
  let last : Fin b.dim := ⟨b.dim - 1, Nat.sub_lt hd (by omega)⟩
  let l : B →ₗ[R] R := b.basis.coord last
  let q : B →ₗ[R] Module.Dual R B := (LinearMap.mul R B).compr₂ l
  let c := b.basis.dualBasis.reindex Fin.revPerm
  let M := LinearMap.toMatrix b.basis c q
  have hentry (i j : Fin b.dim) : M i j = l (b.gen ^ ((j : ℕ) + i.rev)) := by
    simp [M, c, q, LinearMap.toMatrix_apply, Basis.dualBasis_repr,
      l, Basis.repr_reindex, b.basis_eq_pow, ← pow_add]
  have hcoeff {k : ℕ} (hk : k < b.dim) : l (b.gen ^ k) =
      if k = b.dim - 1 then 1 else 0 := by
    change b.basis.repr (b.gen ^ k) last = _
    rw [← b.basis_eq_pow ⟨k, hk⟩, Basis.repr_self]
    simp only [Finsupp.single_apply, last, Fin.mk.injEq]
  have hentry' (i j : Fin b.dim) (hji : j ≤ i) : M i j = if j = i then 1 else 0 := by
    have := i.isLt
    rw [hentry, hcoeff (by simp only [Fin.val_rev]; have := Fin.le_def.1 hji; omega)]
    exact if_congr (by simp only [Fin.val_rev, Fin.ext_iff]; have := Fin.le_def.1 hji; omega)
      rfl rfl
  have htri : M.IsUpperTriangular := fun i j hij => by
    simp [hentry' i j hij.le, (ne_of_lt hij : j ≠ i)]
  have hdiag (i : Fin b.dim) : M i i = 1 := by
    simp [hentry' i i le_rfl]
  have hdet : IsUnit M.det := by
    rw [Matrix.det_of_isUpperTriangular htri]
    simp [hdiag]
  exact (LinearEquiv.ofIsUnitDet (f := q) (v := b.basis) (v' := c) hdet).bijective

/-- **An algebra with a power basis carries a perfect multiplication pairing.**  Some
`R`-linear functional `l` on `B` makes `x ↦ l(x · -)` a bijection `B → Hom_R(B, R)`.

For positive dimension this is `Dualizing.powerBasis_bijective_mul_topCoord`; a power basis of
dimension `0` forces `B` to be the zero ring, where `l = 0` already works. -/
theorem powerBasis_exists_bijective_mul_compr₂ (b : PowerBasis R B) :
    ∃ l : B →ₗ[R] R, Function.Bijective ((LinearMap.mul R B).compr₂ l) := by
  by_cases hd : 0 < b.dim
  · exact ⟨_, powerBasis_bijective_mul_topCoord b hd⟩
  · have hempty : IsEmpty (Fin b.dim) := ⟨fun i => by have := i.isLt; omega⟩
    let := hempty
    have : Subsingleton B := b.basis.repr.injective.subsingleton
    exact ⟨0, Function.bijective_of_subsingleton' _⟩

/-- **An algebra with a power basis is self-dual.**  This is
`Dualizing.powerBasis_exists_bijective_mul_compr₂` read as the predicate
`Dualizing.IsSelfDualAlgebra`. -/
theorem isSelfDualAlgebra_of_powerBasis (b : PowerBasis R B) : IsSelfDualAlgebra R B :=
  powerBasis_exists_bijective_mul_compr₂ b

/-- **An algebra with a power basis is its own `R`-linear dual, as a module over itself.**
For `B` an `R`-algebra with a power basis,

`B ≅ Hom_R(B, R)`

as `B`-modules, where the right-hand side carries the `B`-action by precomposition with
multiplication, i.e. is `ModuleCat.coextendScalars (algebraMap R B)` applied to `R`.

This is `Dualizing.IsSelfDualAlgebra.iso` at the self-duality
`Dualizing.isSelfDualAlgebra_of_powerBasis`: the isomorphism is `x ↦ l(x · -)` for a functional
`l` making multiplication a perfect pairing, and for positive dimension one may take
`l = δ_{d-1}`, the coefficient of the top power basis vector. -/
noncomputable def powerBasisSelfDualIso (b : PowerBasis R B) :
    ModuleCat.of B B ≅ (ModuleCat.coextendScalars (algebraMap R B)).obj (ModuleCat.of R R) :=
  (isSelfDualAlgebra_of_powerBasis b).iso

/-- **`R[x]/(f)` is its own `R`-linear dual for a monic `f`.**  Let `f ∈ R[x]` be monic.  Then

`R[x]/(f) ≅ Hom_R(R[x]/(f), R)`

as `R[x]/(f)`-modules.  `AdjoinRoot f` is `R[X] ⧸ Ideal.span {f}`, and the right-hand side is
`Hom_R(-, R)` in the form `ModuleCat.coextendScalars (algebraMap R (AdjoinRoot f))` applied to
`R`.

This is the generator `δ_{d-1}` of the proof of [Stacks, Tag 0C0G]: it is what turns derived
coextension of scalars along `R → R[x]/(f)` into derived extension of scalars, and so lets the
finite case of that tag be reduced to the surjective one.  A monic `f` of degree `d` gives
`AdjoinRoot f` the power basis `1, x, …, x^{d-1}` of `Polynomial.Monic.powerBasis'`, and the
functional is the coefficient of `x^{d-1}`. -/
noncomputable def adjoinRootSelfDualIso (f : Polynomial R) (hf : f.Monic) :
    ModuleCat.of (AdjoinRoot f) (AdjoinRoot f) ≅
      (ModuleCat.coextendScalars (algebraMap R (AdjoinRoot f))).obj (ModuleCat.of R R) :=
  powerBasisSelfDualIso (AdjoinRoot.powerBasis' hf)

/-- **`R[x]/(f)` is a self-dual `R`-algebra for a monic `f`.**  The predicate behind
`Dualizing.adjoinRootSelfDualIso`, in the form that `Dualizing.IsSelfDualAlgebra.trans` composes
along a tower of monic root extensions. -/
theorem isSelfDualAlgebra_adjoinRoot (f : Polynomial R) (hf : f.Monic) :
    IsSelfDualAlgebra R (AdjoinRoot f) :=
  isSelfDualAlgebra_of_powerBasis (AdjoinRoot.powerBasis' hf)

end Dualizing

end
